#%PAM-1.0
# W Linux — polkit authentication stack (see quickshell-auth.md).
#
# Fingerprint first (a no-op if fprintd is not configured or no finger is
# enrolled), then the password.
#
# The first line exists so the SECOND one can be skipped on demand. While the W
# auth card waits on the reader it offers "Use password"; pam_fprintd owns the
# conversation until its own max-tries/timeout expire (30s by default), so
# without this the button could only reveal the field and let the typed password
# sit queued. GDM solves this by having two PAM services and choosing one —
# polkit cannot, because polkit-agent-helper-1 hardcodes the service name
# "polkit-1", so the choice has to be made inside this single stack.
#
#   gate exit 0     → success=ignore → continue here, pam_fprintd runs as always
#   gate exit != 0  → default=1      → jump over pam_fprintd → the password chain
#
# Every failure of the gate (missing, unreadable, not executable) also lands on
# `default`, i.e. on "ask for the password" — it can never skip authentication.
#
# ⚠️ The gate does NOT run as the user, and cannot see their runtime directory.
# Since polkit 126 this stack runs inside polkit-agent-helper@.service — a
# socket-activated ROOT unit with ProtectHome=yes, which hides /home, /root and
# /run/user. The flag therefore lives in /run/w/fp (sticky, tmpfiles.d/w.conf)
# and the account is taken from $PAM_USER. See /usr/lib/w/w-fp-gate for the flag
# it reads and why it is safe.
#
# ── The password chain below is a COPY of system-auth's auth section, not an
# include. Since polkit 126/127, cancelling the agent's card does not kill the
# helper: the socket-activated polkit-agent-helper-1 only learns of the cancel
# when pam_unix next talks to a dead socket. Upstream system-auth maps that to
# `default=bad`, so pam_faillock authfail tallied every dismissed card (measured
# on a live reader: one entry per cancel, TWO per "Use password" then Cancel —
# three dismissals locked the password prompt for ten minutes while the
# fingerprint kept working). A dismissal is not an attempt: the user presented
# nothing. Hence exactly ONE deliberate delta from the pinned upstream snapshot
# (scripts/check/pam.sh + tests/fixtures/system-auth.auth):
#
#   pam_unix:  [success=1 default=bad]
#           →  [success=1 authtok_err=die conv_err=die default=bad]
#
# The code a dead conversation actually arrives as is PAM_AUTHTOK_ERR, not
# PAM_CONV_ERR: pam_unix reads the token via pam_get_authtok(), which maps a
# failed prompt to PAM_AUTHTOK_ERR ("Authentication token manipulation error" —
# the measured final rc of every tallied cancel). `authtok_err=die` therefore
# fails the stack on the spot: authfail never runs (no tally), authsucc never
# runs (no reset), pam_authenticate returns an error at once. `conv_err=die` is
# kept as a belt for paths that propagate the raw code. `die` rather than a jump
# because for pam_authenticate a jump counts as `ignore` (pam.conf(5)) and the
# stack could then complete successfully with no credential presented at all.
# Everything else in the copied block must stay byte-identical to the snapshot —
# that is what `scripts/check.sh --pam` enforces, including a live drift sensor
# against the host's own pambase.
#
# No `timeout=` override here on purpose: 30s is the honest window for someone
# reaching for the sensor. The stale-reader problem it used to paper over is
# handled where it belongs — w-authd ends the abandoned verification outright by
# restarting fprintd (50-w-fprintd-abort.rules), which pam_fprintd treats as an
# immediate give-up without spending a try.
auth       [success=ignore default=1]   pam_exec.so quiet /usr/lib/w/w-fp-gate
auth       sufficient   pam_fprintd.so
auth       required                    pam_faillock.so      preauth
-auth      [success=2 default=ignore]  pam_systemd_home.so
auth       [success=1 authtok_err=die conv_err=die default=bad]  pam_unix.so  try_first_pass nullok
auth       [default=die]               pam_faillock.so      authfail
auth       optional                    pam_permit.so
auth       required                    pam_env.so
auth       required                    pam_faillock.so      authsucc
account    include      system-auth
session    include      system-auth
